Z||000000||difranco||10-11-1999||06:01 PM||||Changed the fule pump & timing chain and gears last month everything was working fine.  Today driving down the highway car backfired and died.  Will not start.  After checking found no spark from the coil.  rotor is turning.  Could it be the ignition module under the dist. cap?||||reg||8
Z||000001||fiero56||10-11-1999||07:34 PM||||I would suggest replacing the coil first, then if still no spark, return the new coil and go on to the next item.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||10-11-1999||09:01 PM||||That assumes you can return electrical parts. Many stores don't allow this.<P>You can have the distributor module tested. You might be able to test the pickup coil in the dist too.<P>Place a meter across the 2 PRIMARY terminals on the coil. Crank the motor a little bit. If you don't see the needle on the meter bounce at all then it's likely not the coil. (DON'T mess with the secondary, high voltage, wire!) watch the meter carefully it twitches quick. if it goes the wrong way reverse the leads.<P>find the tach filter and disconnect it. Try starting the car. This device will short out the coil when it fails. If it starts, the filter is gone.<P>If it still won't start, and the meter across the coil won't bounce at all, then the module or pickup are bad.||||reg||1
Z||000003||difranco||10-12-1999||08:27 AM||||Had the ignition module tested it was bad.  Replaced and I'm back on the road.  <P>Ogre, where is the tach filter located?||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||10-12-1999||09:08 AM||||the filter is a small metal can bolted to the block. usually near the distributor.... may be near the thermostat neck.||||reg||1
Z||000005||BENGAL4||10-12-1999||09:28 AM||||I always carry a spare igniton module in my GT. It seems they fail pretty easy and at the worst times. It only took 1 time for me to be stuck in the middle of nowhere to convince me to carry a spare.||||reg||10
Z||000006||88 Fiero||10-12-1999||10:25 AM||||Since most of the problem starts with the ignition module. Have anyone thought of relocating this module out of the distributor...I friend did this on his V6 Fiero... I ignition module is relocated in the trunk compartment and it has it's own wiring...weird but he got reason's about it...Now he doesn't have to worry about getting it fried inside the distributor...It really get hot in there...||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||10-12-1999||11:26 AM||||When you install new modules make sure you use fresh heat sink compound. this stuff improves the heat disipation of the module tremendously. If your new module doesn't come with it, you can get it at Radio shack or any other electronics supplier. just clean off the mounting area on the distributor, apply a small gob of compound to the distributor side of the module and screw it down. the compound will spread to fill an gaps by itself.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Dan||10-12-1999||11:58 AM|||| On trying to determine if a pickup coil is bad, you will not have any spark or fuel.||||reg||1
